{"name":"Greek Flag Blue","hex":"#0d5eaf","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"greek","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative letter-case form of Greek (“nonsense writing or talk; gibberish”).","Alternative letter-case form of Greek (“anal sex”)."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To display a placeholder (instead of text), especially to optimize speed in displaying text that would be too small to read.","To fill a template with nonsense text (particularly the Lorem ipsum), so that form can be focused on instead of content."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/greek"},{"term":"flag","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A piece of cloth, often decorated with an emblem, used as a visual signal or symbol.","The design that could be placed on a flag, typically a rectangular graphic that is used to represent an entity (like a country, organisation or group of people) or an idea."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To furnish or deck out with flags.","To mark with a flag, especially to indicate the importance of something."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/flag"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Flag of Greece","blocks":[{"text":"The national flag of Greece, popularly referred to as the Blue-and-White or the Cyan-and-White, is officially recognised by Greece as one of its national symbols and has 9 horizontal stripes of alternating blue and white. There is a blue canton in the upper hoist-side corner bearing a white cross; the cross symbolises Christianity. The blazon of the flag is azure, four bars argent; on a canton of the field a Greek cross throughout of the second. The shade of blue used in the flag has varied throughout its history, from light blue to dark blue, the latter being increasingly used since the late 1960s. It was officially adopted by the First National Assembly at Epidaurus on 13 January 1822."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5c/Flag_of_Greece.svg/330px-Flag_of_Greece.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flag_of_Greece","wikidata":"Q129132"},{"title":"Flag","blocks":[{"text":"A flag is a piece of fabric with distinctive colours and design, often flown from a pole and used for symbolic, signalling, or decorative purposes. The term flag is also used to refer to the graphic design employed on such pieces of fabric. The study of flags is known as \"vexillology\" from the Latin vexillum, meaning \"flag\" or \"banner\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/United_Nations_Flags_-_cropped.jpg/330px-United_Nations_Flags_-_cropped.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flag","wikidata":"Q14660"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/Graeco-Ottoman_banner.png/500px-Graeco-Ottoman_banner.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/Graeco-Ottoman_banner.png/500px-Graeco-Ottoman_banner.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The merchant vessel Panagia tis Hydras, built 1793, flying the Graeco-Ottoman flag","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGraeco-Ottoman_banner.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/73/Agios_Nikolaos_and_Poseidon.jpg/500px-Agios_Nikolaos_and_Poseidon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/73/Agios_Nikolaos_and_Poseidon.jpg/500px-Agios_Nikolaos_and_Poseidon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Agios Nikolaos (1797) and Poseidon (1815), belonging to the Anargyros brothers from Spetses, flying variants of the Russian flag","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAgios_Nikolaos_and_Poseidon.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a2/%CE%92%CF%81%CF%85%CE%B6%CE%AC%CE%BA%CE%B7%CF%82%2C_%CE%98%CE%B5%CF%8C%CE%B4%CF%89%CF%81%CE%BF%CF%82_-_%CE%9F_%CE%A0%CE%B1%CE%BB%CE%B1%CE%B9%CF%8E%CE%BD_%CE%A0%CE%B1%CF%84%CF%81%CF%8E%CE%BD_%CE%93%CE%B5%CF%81%CE%BC%CE%B1%CE%BD%CF%8C%CF%82_%CE%B5%CF%85%CE%BB%CE%BF%CE%B3%CE%B5%CE%AF_%CF%84%CE%B7_%CF%83%CE%B7%CE%BC%CE%B1%CE%AF%CE%B1_%CF%84%CE%B7%CF%82_%CE%95%CF%80%CE%B1%CE%BD%CE%AC%CF%83%CF%84%CE%B1%CF%83%CE%B7%CF%82%2C_1865.jpg/120px-%CE%92%CF%81%CF%85%CE%B6%CE%AC%CE%BA%CE%B7%CF%82%2C_%CE%98%CE%B5%CF%8C%CE%B4%CF%89%CF%81%CE%BF%CF%82_-